\u026ad/; also known colloquially as <b>water bears</b>, or <b>moss piglets</b>) are water-dwelling, eight-legged, segmented micro-animals. They were first discovered by the German zoologist Johann August Ephraim Goeze in 1773. The name <i><b>Tardigrada</b></i> (meaning \"slow stepper\") was given three years later by the Italian biologist Lazzaro Spallanzani. They have been found everywhere: from mountaintops to the deep sea and mud volcanoes; from tropical rain forests to the Antarctic.", "vision": false}]}